---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { repoLink } from "../lib/data";
import release from "../../../docs/release/package-channel-readiness-matrix.json";
const version = release.selected_v0_1_0_release_tag.slice(1);
---

<SiteLayout title="About" description="What makes ShardLoom different: encoded execution, reusable preparation, exact aggregation, and native results." path="/about" active="about">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Less repeated work. One native engine.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      ShardLoom is a published technical preview for local Vortex-native compute. Python, SQL, and CLI calls share native execution families, reusable preparation, and explicit output boundaries.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href="/start">Install and run</a>
      <a class="button" href="/compute-engine-flow">View compute flow</a>
      <a class="button ghost" href={repoLink("README.md")}>Open README</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Differentiators</p>
      <h2>Keep useful structure through the whole route.</h2>
      <p class="narrow">
        The engine avoids reads, decoding, copies, and materialization where the admitted operation allows it. These capabilities have specific type and operator limits; the <a href="/field-guide/runtime-and-io">runtime and I/O guide</a> makes those limits explicit.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <article class="card"><span class="badge">Work avoidance</span><h3>Use what the data already knows.</h3><p>Exact metadata can answer supported queries before row reads. Statistics prune segments; encoded kernels use dictionary codes, values, and repetition counts. Top-K routes defer payload gathering until selection.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="badge">Exact aggregation</span><h3>Exploit repetition without approximating the answer.</h3><p>Weighted counts, fused SUM/AVG work, complete key partitions, and delayed measures serve admitted grouping routes while preserving exact DISTINCT and grouping equality.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="badge">Preparation</span><h3>Reuse an artifact only while its identity holds.</h3><p>Unchanged compatibility inputs can reuse schema-bound Vortex preparation. Source and artifact generation checks reject detected changes. Native Vortex preparation preserves the input layout.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="badge">Resident execution</span><h3>Retain setup, renew query state.</h3><p>Prepared sessions retain source handles and lowering for supported families. Python contexts reuse a local worker to avoid per-call process startup; each query gets fresh execution state.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="badge">Native ownership</span><h3>Carry results into their next consumer.</h3><p>Supported results own Vortex arrays and memory credits. Bounded Rust composition can consume them directly; flat result exports reach shared writers without rerunning the query or reparsing serialized JSON.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="badge">Local I/O</span><h3>Choose a format at the boundary.</h3><p>Vortex, Parquet, Arrow IPC, Avro, ORC, CSV, JSON, and JSONL use explicit adapters and writers. Vortex remains the highest-fidelity persistence target; compatibility export exposes type and metadata limits.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="badge">Resources</span><h3>Make pressure and ownership visible.</h3><p>Bounded queues, reservations, cancellation cleanup, and selected native spill routes govern admitted work. PulseWeave and typed capillary work units expose control decisions; broader adaptive scheduling remains scoped work.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="badge">No fallback</span><h3>Know which engine executed the work.</h3><p>Structured route evidence exposes fallback_attempted=false and external_engine_invoked=false. Unsupported work returns a deterministic diagnostic instead of silently delegating to another query engine.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-grid">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">Available today</p><h2>A technical preview you can install.</h2></div>
    <div>
      <p>GitHub pre-release, PyPI, TestPyPI, and Homebrew publication is verified for {version}. Package availability does not imply production support or broad SQL/DataFrame parity.</p>
      <p>General relational completion, broader spill, and a shared all-I/O layout-optimization policy remain unfinished. General computed-result exports are bounded, and an existing Vortex file is not automatically rebuilt into a new optimized layout.</p>
      <p><a href={repoLink("docs/release/v1-local-source-package-release.md")}>Publication evidence</a> · <a href={repoLink("docs/release/public-status-matrix.md")}>Support matrix</a> · <a href="/field-guide/limitations">Current limitations</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Timing</p>
      <h2>Hot runtime is separated from proof publication.</h2>
      <p class="narrow">
        Benchmark route totals name their timing surface so proof-heavy rows cannot silently redefine hot route timing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="surface-grid">
      <article class="surface-card">
        <span class="badge">hot_runtime</span>
        <h3>Runtime route surface</h3>
        <p>Default performance view. Result-sink replay and human evidence rendering are excluded unless the route formula explicitly includes them.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="surface-card">
        <span class="badge">full_replay_proof</span>
        <h3>Machine replay proof</h3>
        <p>Shows runtime plus replay or sink proof work when that row declares the cost as part of the proof surface.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="surface-card">
        <span class="badge">publication_proof</span>
        <h3>Publication-proof route</h3>
        <p>Includes result-sink and human evidence rendering when published evidence requires it. This is intentionally slower and labeled as proof work.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="strip">
      <div class="metric"><strong>ClickBench</strong><span>public comparison surface</span></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>local</strong><span>engineering benchmark artifacts</span></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>explicit</strong><span>benchmark claim gate</span></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>named</strong><span>route and dataset required</span></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>false</strong><span>runtime fallback delegation</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
